  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 226 AM EDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nIf today is the start of meteorological fall, Mother Nature was not \ngiven the memo. An oppressive 500 mb ridge will be parked over much \nof the Mid South through this week, reaching above the 90th\npercentile and flirting with the maximum values for this time of \nyear. As a consequence, high temperatures down here at the surface\nare increasing as we head into the middle of the week, with highs\nin the mid to upper 90s commonplace for much of the Tennessee \nValley and northern Plateau. Wednesday and Thursday carry a 45% \nchance of hitting 100F at Chattanooga, and Thursday the NBM has a \n50% chance in Knoxville of doing the same. Heat indices will climb\nas well, with maximum values around 105F in the middle of the \nweek. Forecast temperatures alone will flirt with a couple of \ndaily records, namely on the 2nd and 3rd of September.\n\nDaily Record High Maximum Temperatures\n\nDate    Chattanooga    Knoxville      Tri-Cities     Oak Ridge           \n09-01   100(1957)      100(1951)      98(1953)       101(1953)      \n09-02   100(2011)      98(2011)       96(1943)       99(1953)       \n09-03   100(1954)      98(2011)       96(2011)       99(2011)       \n09-04   102(1954)      101(1954)      99(1954)       100(1954)      \n\nThe upper ridge will get shunted to the west as we go into the \nweekend. The degree of heat relief will depend on how significant of \nan east coast trough we see, the GFS seems to have a more outlier \nsignificant solution versus the Euro/EC-AIFS. Any weakening should \nyield some relief outright, and maybe help initiate more storms to \nprovide quicker diurnal relief.\n\nRain chances are slim for much of this week. The overall oppressive \nridge won't lend much to vertical CAPE profiles this week, and we \nshould see even less convection today as a result of the \nincreasingly hotter airmass. The degree to which we see more \nwidespread low chances of showers/thunderstorms over the weekend \ninto next week will depend on the depth of the upper trough and how \nfar west the upper ridge is pushed. For now much of the area has a \nlow rain chance returning each afternoon beginning on Saturday, when \neven the Euro has a weak northwest flow shortwave to help spark \nbetter coverage. Until then, the most significant weather hazard is \nthe anomalously high temperatures, with fairly regular humidity \npushing the combined heat indices to near or at heat advisory \nthresholds for a couple days this week.",
